# Expanded rows 15-28
BATCH2 = [
(15, "SpotFi finds exactly where a WiFi device is indoors using normal WiFi signals. GPS does not work inside buildings, yet indoor location is needed for navigation in malls, hospitals, and smart homes. The goal is very accurate location using only one or two access points instead of many.",
"WiFi signals bounce off walls and furniture so many copies arrive mixed together, and cheap cards add random phase errors. It is very hard to tell which signal came straight from the device and which ones are just reflections.",
"SpotFi combined time and angle information from channel measurements across antennas and subcarriers. It used a joint estimation method to separate direct and reflected paths, then picked the direct path for location with only a few access points and normal cards.",
"Reached about 40 cm median error using real Intel 5300 cards, far better than previous RSS and angle-only methods, in offices and classrooms with only one or two access points and no extra hardware.",
"Prototype Validated", "Must-read for indoor localization background. Cite it as the standard super-resolution joint angle-and-time method behind later tracking, gesture, and vital-sign work.",
"Test with wider 802.11ac/ax bandwidths, combine with deep learning for noisy rooms, and try real-time phone tracking with less calibration."),
(16, "ArrayTrack tracks WiFi devices indoors in real time with very low error. Normal WiFi location needs many access points and slow scanning. The goal is fast, accurate tracking for shops, offices, and games using only WiFi signals already in the room.",
"Indoor signals reflect everywhere so the true direction hides among false peaks. Normal methods need many packets and heavy search, which is slow. Real-time tracking needs quick answers even when people move fast and signals change constantly.",
"ArrayTrack built a special access point with many antennas and fast processing. It redesigned angle-of-arrival estimation to suppress reflections and false peaks, using spatial smoothing and fast algorithms so updates run quickly with only a few packets per move.",
"About 23 cm median error in offices with only a few packets in real time, beating RSS systems by a large margin and showing many antennas plus smart processing give reliable tracking.",
"Prototype Validated", "Must-read for multi-antenna localization history. Cite it to show hardware scaling improves accuracy before SpotFi cut the antenna count; useful for cost/accuracy/real-time comparison.",
"Reduce antenna count and cost, test with commodity routers and phones, and handle crowds, non-line-of-sight rooms, and long-term drift."),
(17, "Phaser wants accurate indoor location with cheap hardware and few access points. Most accurate systems need many antennas or careful clock sync. The goal is a simple system any normal WiFi network can use without expensive changes or extra sensors.",
"WiFi devices are not clock synchronized, so phase measurements drift and look random, and signals bounce off walls mixing many paths. Getting a clean angle or distance from cheap cards with two or three antennas is very difficult without shared clocks.",
"Phaser shared a common clock across antennas using splitters and calibrated hardware. It measured phase differences carefully to get true angle of arrival, then combined angles from a few nodes with commodity cards and small arrays.",
"About 1-2 meter median error in offices with only commodity Atheros cards and a few nodes, proving calibrated phase beats signal strength without heavy fingerprint training or maps.",
"Prototype Validated", "Optional but useful for calibration background. Cite it to explain phase calibration and clock sharing before trusting angle, Doppler, or vital-sign features.",
"Remove wired clock sharing with wireless sync, test larger buildings and moving users, and combine calibrated phase with Doppler and learning for single-AP accuracy."),
(18, "RT-Fall detects when an elderly person falls at home using WiFi, without cameras or wearables. Falls are dangerous if no one notices quickly. The goal is real-time, private fall detection through walls and across rooms using normal home WiFi.",
"Daily actions like sitting, picking things up, or lying down look similar to falls in WiFi signals. Homes differ in layout, furniture, and people. A system must avoid false alarms, run in real time, and still catch rare falls rarely seen in training.",
"RT-Fall used channel amplitude and phase change patterns during motion, extracted fall-like features, and trained a classifier separating falls from normal activities, with segmentation and thresholds for real-time alerts, tested with volunteers doing falls and daily actions.",
"Around 91 percent detection with low false alarms in home tests, running in real time on commodity Intel 5300 cards across rooms, showing WiFi alone can give useful fall alerts. (TMC 2017; early access 2016.)",
"Prototype Validated", "Must-read for WiFi fall detection. Cite it as an early real-time CSI fall system with clear metrics, and compare its handcrafted features with your Doppler, segmentation, or deep-learning method.",
"Test with real elderly people and long-term data, cut false alarms for fast sitting and pets, and add multi-person rooms, edge deployment, and caregiver alerts."),
(19, "PhaseFi locates people indoors even when they carry no phone or device (device-free passive tracking), useful for security, elder care, and smart homes where the person holds nothing that sends WiFi signals.",
"Without a device the system sees only weak body reflections mixed with strong wall reflections. Signal strength changes are noisy and room-dependent. Training a map for every room takes time, and maps break when furniture moves.",
"PhaseFi used calibrated CSI phase difference instead of noisy amplitude. It built a fingerprint map of phase features at known positions, then trained a deep network on location patterns, matching new measurements to learned positions with no device on the person. (GLOBECOM 2015 / TMC 2016.)",
"About 1 meter accuracy in lab and living-room tests. Calibrated phase beat amplitude fingerprints and stayed more stable over time with Intel 5300 cards, showing deep learning improves passive location with small training sets.",
"Prototype Validated", "Must-read for device-free localization and phase use. Cite it for calibrated phase fingerprints and deep models, contrasting fingerprint methods with model-based tracking like Widar.",
"Cut training with transfer learning across rooms, handle multiple people and moving furniture, and test larger homes in real time with cheap routers."),
(20, "Chronos locates WiFi devices with high accuracy using only one access point. Normal systems need three or more for triangulation. The goal is precise indoor location for drones, robots, and smart spaces without installing much new infrastructure.",
"One access point gives limited angle and distance clues. WiFi clocks drift and bandwidth is narrow, so time-of-flight looks blurry, and reflections add false paths. Centimeter accuracy from one cheap link seems almost impossible without ultra-wideband hardware.",
"Chronos hopped across many WiFi frequency bands and stitched them into a wide virtual bandwidth. It measured time-of-flight very finely from combined channels, then fused distance with angle and fixed clock errors by two-way exchange between device and access point.",
"About 65 cm median error, often decimeter accuracy, in offices with only one access point and commodity cards using frequency hopping, far better than RSS and narrowband time methods, running in seconds per fix.",
"Prototype Validated", "Must-read for single-AP high-accuracy ranging. Cite it for multi-band splicing and time-of-flight ideas, and to explain bandwidth limits in your CSI work.",
"Make hopping faster for moving targets, support many devices at once, and test crowded spectrum with 802.11ax bands, phones, and IoT tags."),
(21, "Widar tracks human walking and location using WiFi with no device on the body. Cameras need light and invade privacy. The goal is estimating walking direction, distance, and path from body reflections so homes and offices can follow people passively and safely.",
"The human reflection is weak and mixed with static wall signals. The same walk looks different across rooms and directions. Speed and heading must be estimated from noisy Doppler shifts without knowing the starting position or carrying any sensor.",
"Widar estimated velocity and direction from Doppler and angle changes in CSI. It removed static paths, tracked the moving body reflection over time, and built a path from speed and heading estimates, tested walking in classrooms and offices. NOTE: superseded by Widar2.0/3.0.",
"Median tracking error around 38 cm and direction error under 10 degrees in room tests, with no wearables or cameras, proving passive Doppler tracking possible but still needing room-dependent tuning and good line-of-sight.",
"Prototype Validated", "Optional because superseded, but cite for history as the first Widar passive-tracking step before Widar2.0; one line showing progress from velocity estimation to joint tracking.",
"See Widar2.0/3.0: reduce room dependence, handle multiple people, improve start-position estimation, and add real-time phone or router versions."),
(22, "Widar2.0 wants device-free human tracking that works in new rooms without retraining. Widar and fingerprint systems break when moved to another building. The goal is tracking that learns motion physics, not room maps, so it works across homes, offices, and halls.",
"Each room has different walls and paths, so raw signals change completely, and Doppler is ambiguous without exact geometry. A system must separate body motion from room reflections and estimate position, speed, and direction together from weak dynamic signals alone.",
"Widar2.0 modeled how body position, velocity, and orientation create Doppler, angle, and distance changes. It jointly estimated all motion states from CSI without room fingerprints on commodity cards, testing the same model in many rooms, corridors, and offices. (MobiCom 2018.)",
"Median error around 75 cm across different rooms with no retraining. Tracked walking paths, turns, and U-shapes, generalizing far better than fingerprint methods and proving model-based tracking moves across environments with one setup.",
"Prototype Validated", "Must-read for cross-room passive tracking. Cite it as the key model-based upgrade over fingerprinting and Widar1, justifying Doppler-plus-geometry features and environment-independent design.",
"Push below 50 cm, handle multiple people, add gesture orientation, faster initialization, phone-based real-time versions, and crowded non-line-of-sight homes."),
(23, "Widar3.0 recognizes hand gestures using WiFi in any room without new training. Older gesture systems fail in new places because signals depend on walls and furniture. The goal is one model working across users, rooms, and devices for smart-home control. (MobiSys 2019, DOI 10.1145/3307334.3326081; 479 citations.)",
"The same hand move creates very different raw signals in different rooms. Speed, hand size, and position vary too. A model trained in one lab usually drops sharply in a new living room. Learning only room-independent motion features is very difficult.",
"Widar3.0 extracted body-coordinate velocity profiles describing hand motion itself, not room paths. It trained a deep network on these motion features using data from many rooms, released a large gesture dataset, and showed transfer to unseen rooms, users, and positions.",
"About 92.7 percent in-domain and 82.6-92.4 percent cross-domain accuracy on gestures, beating raw-CSI models (raw CSI ~40 percent, DFS ~78 percent in cross-environment cases) with thousands of samples across rooms, positions, and orientations.",
"Open Benchmark", "Must-read for cross-domain gesture recognition. Cite it for velocity-profile features and public dataset use, and as your baseline for domain-independent sensing and in-room versus cross-room comparison.",
"Add more gestures, continuous signing, and two-hand moves. Reduce data needs with few-shot learning, deploy on edge routers and phones, and test busy homes with interference."),
(24, "SignFi recognizes sign language and gestures using WiFi without cameras or gloves. Cameras fail in dark rooms and raise privacy fears. The goal is accurate daily sign recognition at home and in labs using only normal WiFi signals bouncing off hands and arms. (SenSys 2018.)",
"Sign moves are small, fast, and similar to each other. Tiny hand-shape changes hide in noise. Different users perform the same sign differently, and room reflections change the pattern, making reliable classification hard with limited training data.",
"SignFi collected CSI from Intel 5300 cards during many repeated signs and gestures. It cleaned signals and trained classifiers on amplitude and phase patterns, testing lab and home sets with many users and signs, and released data and methods for fair comparison.",
"About 86-94 percent accuracy on 150 signs and 276 gestures in lab tests (8280 instances); home accuracy lower but useful. Proved WiFi can separate many fine hand signs and gave the field a large public dataset.",
"Open Benchmark", "Must-read for gesture or sign work. Cite it for dataset scale, lab-versus-home gap, and multi-sign baseline; use it to justify your gesture set size, user splits, and cross-user protocol.",
"Improve home and cross-user robustness with domain adaptation, add continuous sentence signing and real-time feedback, and test on cheap routers with deaf-community users."),
(25, "FarSense senses human motion and breathing from far away using WiFi. Most CSI systems only work within a few meters. The goal is reliable sensing across a whole room or hall so one router covers daily activity, falls, and breathing without extra sensors near the bed. (MobiCom 2019.)",
"Distant body reflections are extremely weak. Small moves and breathing create tiny changes buried under noise and strong static paths. Amplitude fades with distance, and phase errors from cheap cards make weak signals even harder to read.",
"FarSense used the ratio of CSI between two antennas to cancel hardware errors and boost weak motion signals. It modeled how this ratio changes with distance and motion, detecting gestures, walking, and breathing at longer ranges on commodity cards across rooms and distances.",
"Extended motion sensing range by several meters and kept high detection where amplitude methods failed, including through-wall and long-room settings with Intel 5300 cards; first through-wall respiration result on commodity WiFi claimed.",
"Prototype Validated", "Must-read for long-range and ratio methods. Cite it to justify CSI-ratio cleaning for weak signals when your work needs far distance, breathing, or through-wall sensing.",
"Push range with directional antennas and 802.11ax, handle multiple weak targets and crowds, and build real-time long-range fall and breathing alerts for large homes and wards."),
(26, "PhaseBeat tracks breathing and heartbeats without touching the body. Wearables are uncomfortable for sleep and long care. The goal is low-cost nightly monitoring using WiFi reflections from the chest so hospitals and homes can watch breathing rate safely and privately. (BHI 2016.)",
"Chest moves only millimeters during breathing and even less for heartbeats. These tiny moves hide in noisy WiFi phase with drift and offsets. People turn, cough, and move in sleep, breaking the weak periodic pattern needed for rate estimation.",
"PhaseBeat used calibrated CSI phase difference between antennas to remove offsets and drift. It selected stable subcarriers, filtered breathing and heartbeat bands, and estimated rates with peak detection, testing lying and sitting people against wearable ground truth.",
"Breathing accuracy around 90 percent with errors under 0.5 beats per minute in good conditions; heartbeat less stable but useful. Worked through blankets and nearby, showing WiFi phase can replace some wearable checks for sleep.",
"Prototype Validated", "Must-read for vital-sign background. Cite it for phase-difference cleaning and rate-estimation pipeline; compare its single-person still-body limits with TensorBeat and multi-person methods.",
"Handle turning, snoring, and multi-person beds. Improve heartbeat accuracy with wider bandwidth, run overnight clinical tests, and build low-false-alarm apnea alerts."),
(27, "TensorBeat monitors breathing for several people at once using WiFi. PhaseBeat works for one still person. The goal is multi-person sleep or ward monitoring without a sensor on each chest, so families and hospitals can watch everyone in a room together. (IPSN 2017.)",
"Signals from multiple chests mix into one WiFi channel. Breathing rates are close, so peaks overlap. People differ in size, position, and motion. Separating two or three weak periodic signals from noisy, drifting CSI is extremely difficult with few antennas.",
"TensorBeat stacked CSI phase differences across antennas, subcarriers, and time into a tensor. It used tensor decomposition to separate each person's breathing signal, then filtered and estimated each rate, testing two to five people sitting, lying, and sleeping with commodity Intel cards.",
"Monitored two to three people with high success and errors under about 0.7 breaths per minute, beating single-signal filtering and spectrum methods in rooms and corridors with only three antennas and no wearables.",
"Prototype Validated", "Must-read for multi-person vital signs. Cite it for tensor separation and phase-difference input; use it to frame multi-target breathing, motion robustness, and antenna-count trade-offs.",
"Scale to more people and larger wards, handle turning, walking, and postures, combine with deep separation and live apnea alarms, and validate overnight against medical monitors."),
(28, "WiCapture tracks tiny hand moves for virtual reality using WiFi. Headsets need precise motion but cameras fail in dark or occluded rooms. The goal is millimeter-level hand trajectory tracking with only WiFi, so VR works anywhere without extra cameras. (NSDI 2017.)",
"Hand moves are only centimeters, creating phase shifts smaller than noise and drift. Multiple body parts reflect at once and the headset itself moves. Turning tiny mixed phase changes into a smooth 3D path with almost no error is very demanding on commodity WiFi.",
"WiCapture tracked phase changes across multiple transmitter-receiver paths to estimate hand displacement. It removed drift and static paths, combined several links for 3D motion, smoothed trajectories over time, and tested with Intel 5300 cards and VR-like hand paths against motion-capture truth.",
"About 0.88 cm median tracking error for hand paths (versus 132 cm for SpotFi-style baselines), working in offices without cameras, showing commodity WiFi can support fine VR-like motion with many paths and careful phase cleaning.",
"Prototype Validated", "Must-read for fine-grained motion tracking. Cite it for phase-to-displacement modeling and multi-link fusion; use it to set accuracy expectations for gesture, writing, or VR-style tracking.",
"Track full 3D finger motion and two hands, reduce setup with fewer access points and live headset integration, and test fast gaming moves, occlusion, and crowds with real-time edge processing."),
]
